    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24139_english_2026-08-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-06-2026-08-06-0059",
    "chunk_index": 59,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "9725579427da18c7fac887a135aab253",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "Lanka Number Portability Services (Guarantee) Limited - LNPS - has taken steps to procure the technical solution and the Letter of Intent is already being issued, but the agreement and the PO are on hold due to the disagreement of one fixed operator in original implementation plan.\n\n3. The Government accepts, without qualification, that the ability to change operators while retaining one\u2019s number is an entitlement due to consumers in a modern telecommunications market and that its delivery is a matter of Government policy commitment rather than of operator discretion.\n\nMobile Number Portability lowers the cost of switching and in doing so, it disciplines price, drives quality of service and rewards operators who innovate. It matters more today than when it was first proposed because a mobile number is no longer only a telephone number, but the identifier by which citizens access banking, government and other digital services and make payment. To be locked to an operator is now to be locked out of a choice, which affects far more than a telephone bill.\n\nThat is precisely why the Government does not regard the present delay as acceptable, and why my Answer to question No. 1 sets out what would follow, if the remaining disagreement is not resolved by the industry.\n\n4. The TRCSL has taken steps to revise the existing Broadband Quality of Service Rules to monitor networks of different technologies. With licensing of the new category of satellite service providers, including Starlink, a national strategy will be introduced for universal broadband connectivity in addition to existing programmes, conducting through fixed and mobile operators to serve the unserved and underserved areas."
  },
